with q as (
  select $astroDyn_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The chapter says the Kirkwood gaps and Saturn's Cassini Division are 'the same phenomenon.' What single physical fact justifies that claim, and what is the one real difference in how the two are cleared?", "options": ["They share the physics of commensurable periods producing coherent, in-phase forcing, but the Cassini Division is cleared by radiation pressure while the Kirkwood gaps are cleared by gravity.", "They are both caused by collisions grinding bodies down at particular distances, differing only in that ring ice is softer than asteroidal rock.", "Both are carved by a mean-motion resonance delivering the perturber's tug at the same orbital phase every cycle so the kicks accumulate; the difference is that in Saturn's dense rings the 2:1 resonance mainly shepherds and confines particles to the gap edge, whereas in the sparse belt the 3:1 is chaotic and pumps eccentricity until the asteroid crosses a planet's orbit and is removed entirely.", "Both arise because the perturber's gravity is simply stronger at those distances, differing only in that Mimas is a moon and Jupiter is a planet."], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "The unifying fact is commensurability: when orbital periods stand in a small-integer ratio, the perturber's tug arrives at the same phase of the smaller body's orbit every cycle and the small kicks add up instead of cancelling. The identical equation a = a_perturber x (T/T_perturber)^(2/3) locates both features. Using Mimas at 185,540 km, the 2:1 resonance falls at 116,900 km, matching the Cassini Division's inner edge near 117,500 km; using Jupiter at 5.204 AU, the 3:1 falls at 2.50 AU, matching that Kirkwood gap. The mechanism ignores scale (120,000 km vs a third of a billion km) and material (centimetre ice vs kilometre rock) because it depends only on the period ratio. The genuine difference is in removal: collisions in the dense rings damp the excitation so the resonance mostly confines particles to the edge, while in the near-empty belt the 3:1 is chaotic and pumps eccentricity until the orbit crosses Mars and Earth and a close encounter ejects the body. Radiation pressure and collisional grinding are not the clearing mechanism, so those distractors fail."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why does a 3:1 period ratio with Jupiter empty a region of the belt, while a period ratio near 3.14:1 does essentially nothing?", "options": ["At 3:1 the asteroid completes exactly three orbits for each Jupiter orbit, so the two return to identical relative geometry and Jupiter's tug is delivered at the same orbital point and direction over and over, building amplitude; at 3.14:1 the geometry never repeats, each close approach falls at a shifting phase, and the tugs point every which way and average to nearly nothing.", "At 3:1 Jupiter is physically closest to the asteroid, so its gravity is strongest, whereas at 3.14:1 the asteroid stays farther from Jupiter.", "The 3:1 ratio makes the asteroid and Jupiter collide periodically, while the 3.14:1 ratio keeps them safely apart.", "Only integer ratios exert any gravitational force at all; a ratio like 3.14:1 is irrational, so Jupiter cannot pull on the asteroid."], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "This is the central resonance idea, moved heliocentrically. Commensurability means coherent forcing: after three asteroid orbits and one Jupiter orbit the pair are back in the same relative positions, so Jupiter's small tug arrives at the same point of the asteroid's orbit, in the same direction, every cycle, and the kicks integrate into a large effect. The chapter's analogy is pushing a child on a swing at the top of every arc versus shoving at random moments: same total force, but one builds amplitude. A ratio like 3.14:1 never brings the geometry back into register, so each approach lands at a slightly different phase and the tugs cancel over many orbits. Jupiter's gravity is not stronger at the resonant distance, the bodies do not collide, and gravity certainly acts at every ratio, integer or not. The distances themselves are found from Kepler's third law: a = 5.204 x (1/3)^(2/3) = 2.50 AU for the 3:1."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The chapter calls the delivery pipeline 'one continuous mechanism from a micronewton force to a museum rock.' Which sequence correctly describes it, with the right role for each stage?", "options": ["A resonance first pumps an asteroid's eccentricity, then the Yarkovsky effect walks it out of the belt, making it a comet that later strikes Earth as a meteorite.", "The Yarkovsky thermal-recoil force (micronewtons, drift like Bennu's ~280 m/yr) slowly walks an asteroid across the belt until it drifts into a resonance; the resonance chaotically pumps eccentricity over ~1 million years until perihelion drops below Mars then Earth, making it a near-Earth object; a small fraction of those that survive atmospheric entry then fall as meteorites.", "Jupiter's gravity directly grabs asteroids from the belt and flings them onto Earth-crossing orbits in a single close encounter, with Yarkovsky playing no role.", "Solar radiation pressure blows small asteroids inward continuously until they reach Earth, and resonances merely slow the process down."], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The pipeline runs in a definite order, each link with a distinct job. Yarkovsky is the slow transport: a spinning asteroid re-radiates heat from its rotated 'afternoon' side, giving a permanent sideways photon shove of a few micronewtons that, because it has a component along the motion, changes the semi-major axis. For Bennu OSIRIS-REx measured about 280 m/yr inward; smaller bodies drift faster because acceleration scales as 1/R. Over tens of millions of years this walks the asteroid into one of the resonances that thread the belt. The resonance is the fast escape: it chaotically pumps eccentricity over roughly a million years until perihelion drops below Mars and then Earth, producing a near-Earth object, a population that survives only a few million years and so must be continuously resupplied. Finally a small fraction on Earth-intersecting orbits that survive entry land as meteorites. The order matters: Yarkovsky transports, the resonance pumps; reversing them (option 1) is wrong, Jupiter does not grab bodies directly in one encounter, and radiation pressure is not the driver."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Jupiter's Trojans are uniformly dark and red. Why does this colour make them evidence for planetary migration rather than just a curiosity?", "options": ["Because red bodies are always older, so the Trojans must be the oldest objects in the solar system and predate the planets.", "Because their red colour proves they are made of iron oxide, which only forms near Jupiter's magnetic field during migration.", "Because the triangular Lagrange points can only trap objects that are already red, so their colour is a selection effect of capture.", "Because if they had formed in place at 5 AU they should share the colours of the neighbouring outer main-belt asteroids, but instead their colours match Kuiper Belt objects that live beyond 40 AU; since a body cannot easily change its intrinsic colour class, they most likely formed in the outer system and were captured at the triangular points when the giant planets migrated."], "answerIndex": 3, "explanation": "The argument is a chain of inference. Surface colour tracks composition and formation environment: the inner belt is stony and neutral, the far outer system dark and red. Had the Trojans condensed at Jupiter's distance and settled locally into L4 and L5, they should resemble the outer main belt next door. Instead they match Kuiper Belt objects that formed beyond Neptune, so a population parked at 5 AU is wearing the colours of one that lives at 40 AU. Because an object cannot easily change its intrinsic colour class, the natural reading is that they are outer-system material transported inward and captured, exactly what a migration scattering the primordial trans-Neptunian disk supplies: as Neptune plowed outward and Jupiter crossed its resonances, the Trojan regions briefly opened and trapped a sample. The swarm is huge (about 13,000 catalogued, plausibly as numerous as the whole main belt), so this is not a fluke. NASA's Lucy (launched 2021, twelve-year tour of both clouds) exists to test capture by measuring composition up close. Colour does not track age, is not proof of iron oxide, and the Lagrange points do not preferentially trap red bodies."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why are the fragile wide binaries of the cold classical Kuiper Belt described as the 'linchpin' that makes the cold classicals a control group, in a way that low inclinations alone are not?", "options": ["A wide binary is barely bound by feeble mutual gravity, so a single close encounter with a massive perturber or a violent scattering would tear it apart; the binaries' survival therefore rules out any violent episode in the population's entire past, a far stronger statement than low inclination, which only constrains the present dynamical state.", "Wide binaries are easier to observe than inclinations, so they give more reliable data even though they carry the same physical information.", "Wide binaries reflect more sunlight, making the cold classicals appear redder, which is the real evidence they never moved.", "The binaries prove the cold classicals are young, because only recently formed pairs can orbit at large separations."], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Low inclination and eccentricity show only that a population is dynamically cold now; in principle a stirred population could have been re-cooled or damped later, so it constrains just the endpoint. A wide binary is a stricter witness because it is destroyed rather than merely heated by a rough history: two comparable bodies loosely bound and separated by tens of thousands of kilometres would be pulled apart by a single close planetary encounter or strong scattering. Their survival therefore rules out any violent episode across the whole 4.5-billion-year past. This is why the cold classicals (roughly 42 to 47 AU, distinctively red, individually pristine) are the control group: a migration model that scatters six other populations must simultaneously leave this narrow strip untouched, which makes the model harder to build and hence falsifiable. New Horizons' 2019 flyby of Arrokoth, a gently assembled contact binary, is a photograph of that undisturbed origin. The binaries are not merely easier to observe, do not cause the red colour, and do not indicate youth; their fragility is precisely the point."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "The chapter argues that migration is 'a theory, not a story' because it explains six unrelated-looking anomalies with one mechanism. List those anomalies and explain, for each, how a single episode of giant-planet migration accounts for it. Then state why explaining many things at once is a higher standard of evidence than explaining any one.", "rubric": "A strong answer names all six anomalies and pairs each with the migration mechanism. (1) The missing mass: the main belt holds only about 5 percent of the Moon's mass, a few ten-thousandths of an Earth, yet the disk there should have built a small planet; migration scatters ~99.9 percent of the mass away. (2) The mixed/scrambled taxonomy: inner belt stony S-types and outer belt carbon-rich, ice-bearing C-types are thoroughly intermixed (C-types found well inside where they could not condense), which a quiet in-place belt would keep neatly sorted; migration injects outer bodies inward and stirs the survivors. (3) The resonant Kuiper Belt populations: Plutinos and kin locked in exact commensurabilities (e.g. 3:2) with Neptune, which a stationary Neptune could never capture; a Neptune sweeping outward drags bodies into its resonances. (4) The scattered disc: icy bodies on enormous eccentric orbits, flung there by something that reached out and threw them, i.e. the migrating planets. (5) The Trojans: dark and red, wearing outer-system (Kuiper) colours at Jupiter's distance, captured at the triangular points during migration. (6) The bombardment record on the Moon and inner planets, read as a spike of impacts hundreds of Myr after formation, delivered as a pulse of impactors during the rearrangement. The synthesis point: ad hoc stories could be invented for each anomaly separately, but a single mechanism that constrains many independent observables at once cannot be freely tuned to each, so agreement across six independent facts is very unlikely to be coincidence. The answer should explicitly frame this as the same standard demanded of the helium-rain argument in the giant-planet chapters: an explanation that constrains many observables simultaneously is a theory, whereas one tuned to a single fact is just a story. Credit for noting that this is what elevates the claim from narrative to theory."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "Trace the delivery pipeline quantitatively and conceptually from the Yarkovsky force to a meteorite, and explain why the near-Earth object population must be in a continuously resupplied steady state rather than a fossil of a single ancient event. Use the chapter's numbers where relevant.", "rubric": "A strong answer describes the physics of the Yarkovsky effect: a spinning asteroid absorbs sunlight and re-radiates heat, but the warmest surface is the 'afternoon' side rotated past noon, so the departing photons carry momentum sideways and give a permanent shove with a component along the direction of motion (prograde rotators pushed forward, retrograde backward). This changes the orbit's energy and thus the semi-major axis. Magnitudes: the force is measured in micronewtons, roughly the weight of a few grains of sand, about ten orders of magnitude weaker than the Sun's gravity on the same rock; Bennu's measured drift (from OSIRIS-REx) is about 280 m/yr inward. Smaller bodies drift faster because the force scales with cross-sectional area (R^2) while mass scales with volume (R^3), so acceleration goes as 1/R. Over tens of millions of years this walks a body tenths of an AU across the belt into a resonance (e.g. the 3:1 at 2.50 AU or the nu6 secular resonance at the inner edge). The resonance chaotically pumps eccentricity over roughly a million years until perihelion drops below Mars then Earth, producing a near-Earth object; a small fraction on Earth-crossing orbits that survive atmospheric entry arrive as meteorites. The steady-state argument: near-Earth objects survive only a few million years (they impact a planet, are ejected, or fall into the Sun), a blink against the ~4.5-billion-year age of the solar system, so the population cannot be primordial and must be continuously replenished. Yarkovsky supplies the slow transport and resonances the fast escape, so the belt leaks through narrow 'drains' at a slow, roughly constant trickle; bodies of a range of sizes arrive continuously (bigger ones over tens of Myr, smaller ones over a few Myr), maintaining a steady state resupplied at about the rate the near-Earth region is depleted, rather than a single ancient pulse. Optional strong detail: the pipeline explains why meteorites are dominated by asteroid families sitting just upstream of a resonance and why the flux is roughly steady. Full credit requires both the mechanism/order (Yarkovsky transports, resonance pumps, then meteorite) and the steady-state logic tied to the short NEO lifetime."}]$astroDyn_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/solar-system/small-bodies/dynamical-architecture', lvl, q.questions, true
